activity-blog.js is the data-fetching/activity-feed module.
blog-horizontal-scroll.js should be the interactive blog browser.

So its job is basically:

Take the blog data already available to the page → build/control the horizontal blog cards → handle scrolling and card interaction → navigate to the selected article.

It should not independently talk to Hygraph, GitHub, or the Markdown files. That separation keeps the modules sane.

Architecture

The overall flow should be:

Markdown blog files
        ↓
blog-index.json
        ↓
activity-blog.js
        ↓
normalized blog-post objects
        ↓
blog-horizontal-scroll.js
        ↓
horizontal blog cards
        ↓
user interaction
        ↓
selected article
        ↓
/blog/<slug>/

For the horizontal-scroll module specifically:

INITIALIZE
   ↓
Find blog section/container
   ↓
Obtain blog post data
   ↓
Validate data
   ↓
Create card for each post
   ↓
Insert cards into horizontal track
   ↓
Attach interaction handlers
   ↓
Enable horizontal navigation
   ↓
Handle resize / responsive behavior
   ↓
User clicks / scrolls / drags
   ↓
Update active/visible card
   ↓
Navigate when card is selected
The important architectural decision

I would give this module two responsibilities:

1. Rendering
2. Interaction

Not data acquisition.

So conceptually:

blog-horizontal-scroll.js

    ├── configuration
    ├── container resolution
    ├── data normalization
    ├── card creation
    ├── track rendering
    ├── scroll controls
    ├── active-card detection
    ├── event handling
    ├── responsive handling
    └── initialization/export
Algorithmic design
1. Initialize

When initBlogHorizontalScroll() is called:

Find the blog horizontal-scroll container.
If it does not exist:
    exit gracefully.

Find the track that will contain cards.
If the track does not exist:
    exit gracefully.

Something like:

BLOG_SECTION
    └── BLOG_TRACK
         ├── CARD
         ├── CARD
         ├── CARD
         └── ...
2. Obtain blog data

This module needs the normalized posts.

There are two clean ways to architect this.

Option A — activity-blog.js exposes the posts
activity-blog.js
       ↓
fetches + normalizes posts
       ↓
controller.state.posts
       ↓
blog-horizontal-scroll.js consumes them

This avoids fetching the Markdown files twice.

This is the architecture I'd prefer.

The normalized object is already something like:

{
    title,
    date,
    description,
    cover,
    url
}

which is exactly what the card needs.

3. Validate posts

Before rendering:

For every post:
    if title missing → skip
    if url missing → skip

You don't need to re-validate everything that activity-blog.js already validated.

This module is primarily concerned with:

title
date
description
cover
url
4. Create the horizontal track

The track is simply a flex/overflow container.

Conceptually:

BLOG SECTION
│
└── TRACK
    │
    ├── CARD 1
    ├── CARD 2
    ├── CARD 3
    ├── CARD 4
    └── CARD N

CSS handles the actual horizontal layout.

JavaScript should not continuously calculate positions unless necessary.

5. Create each card

For every normalized post:

create article
    ↓
add cover image
    ↓
add title
    ↓
add description
    ↓
add publication date
    ↓
make card clickable
    ↓
append card to track

A conceptual card:

┌───────────────────────────┐
│                           │
│        COVER IMAGE        │
│                           │
├───────────────────────────┤
│ Zombie Internet           │
│ How AI-generated content  │
│ is changing the internet  │
│                           │
│ Sep 2, 2026               │
└───────────────────────────┘

The card itself should probably link directly to post.url.

That means JavaScript doesn't need some elaborate:

click card
→ find slug
→ manually construct URL
→ redirect

when the normalized object already contains the destination.

Horizontal interaction

Now the interesting part.

You have a horizontal track:

[ CARD 1 ][ CARD 2 ][ CARD 3 ][ CARD 4 ][ CARD 5 ]
←────────────────────────────────────────────────→

The module can support:

Mouse wheel / trackpad
        ↓
native horizontal scrolling

Previous button
        ↓
scrollBy()

Next button
        ↓
scrollBy()

Click card
        ↓
navigate

That means the browser's native scrolling does most of the heavy lifting.

Don't build a custom physics engine.

Seriously 😂

We don't need:

velocity
acceleration
friction
momentum
drag interpolation
requestAnimationFrame()

unless the design later specifically requires it.

Native horizontal scrolling is already very good.

Active card detection

This is the one piece of JS that can make the component feel polished.

As the user scrolls:

CARD 1    CARD 2    CARD 3
   ↓
determine which card is most visible
   ↓
mark it as active

You could use IntersectionObserver.

Algorithm:

Observe every card.

When visibility changes:
    determine which card has the highest visibility.
    remove "active" from previous card.
    add "active" to new card.

Then CSS can do:

.card.active {
    ...
}

without JavaScript touching styling details.

Navigation controls

Suppose we have:

←      cards      →

The Previous button:

Find currently visible/active card
        ↓
Find previous card
        ↓
scroll that card into view

Next:

Find currently visible/active card
        ↓
Find next card
        ↓
scroll that card into view

Use:

card.scrollIntoView({
    behavior: 'smooth',
    block: 'nearest',
    inline: 'center'
});

rather than manually calculating pixel offsets.

Much simpler.

Responsive behavior

The JS should mostly leave responsiveness to CSS.

For example:

Desktop:
3 cards visible

Tablet:
2 cards visible

Mobile:
1 card visible

CSS handles the card widths.

JavaScript only needs to react to layout changes where necessary.

Potentially:

resize
   ↓
recalculate active card

But don't attach a giant resize computation.

Lifecycle states

I wouldn't make this module as elaborate as activity-blog.js.

It mostly needs:

IDLE
 ↓
INITIALIZED
 ↓
RENDERED
 ↓
INTERACTIVE

Potential failure states:

container missing
track missing
no posts
invalid data

For an empty collection:

"No blog posts available."

That's enough.

Pseudocode

Now the fun part — this is the version I'd actually use as your implementation blueprint.

MODULE blog-horizontal-scroll

CONFIGURATION
    define default section selector
    define default track selector
    define optional previous button selector
    define optional next button selector

FUNCTION initialize(options)

    resolve section/container

    IF container does not exist
        return null

    resolve track

    IF track does not exist
        return null

    obtain normalized blog posts

    IF no posts exist
        render empty state
        return controller

    validate posts

    render cards

    setup scroll interaction

    setup navigation controls

    setup active-card detection

    setup resize handling

    return controller

Then:

FUNCTION getBlogPosts()

    obtain posts from activity-blog controller

    IF posts are unavailable
        return empty array

    return valid posts
Card rendering
FUNCTION renderCards(posts)

    clear track

    FOR EACH post IN posts

        create card element

        create cover image

        IF post.cover exists
            set image source
            set image alt text
            append image
        END IF

        create title element
        set title text
        append title

        IF post.description exists
            create description
            set description text
            append description
        END IF

        create date element
        set formatted date
        append date

        create link / clickable wrapper
        set destination to post.url

        append card to track

    END FOR
Scroll controls
FUNCTION setupNavigation()

    IF previous button exists

        add click listener

        WHEN clicked:

            determine current active card

            determine previous card

            IF previous card exists
                scroll previous card into view
            END IF

    END IF


    IF next button exists

        add click listener

        WHEN clicked:

            determine current active card

            determine next card

            IF next card exists
                scroll next card into view
            END IF

    END IF
Active card detection
FUNCTION setupActiveCardDetection()

    create IntersectionObserver

    observe every card

    WHEN visibility changes

        calculate visibility for each card

        find card with highest visibility

        remove active state from previous card

        add active state to selected card

END FUNCTION
Click handling
FUNCTION setupCardInteractions()

    FOR EACH card

        add click listener

        WHEN card clicked

            obtain card's post URL

            navigate to post URL

        END WHEN

    END FOR

Although, honestly, I'd make the card itself an <a> rather than using a click listener.

Then the browser handles navigation naturally:

<a href="/blog/zombie-internet/">
    card contents
</a>

That is cleaner and more accessible.

Full pseudocode architecture

Putting everything together:

MODULE blog-horizontal-scroll

    CONFIGURATION

        DEFAULT_CONTAINER_SELECTOR
        DEFAULT_TRACK_SELECTOR
        DEFAULT_PREVIOUS_SELECTOR
        DEFAULT_NEXT_SELECTOR


    FUNCTION createCard(post)

        create article/card

        create link

        create cover

        IF cover exists
            add cover
        END IF

        add title

        IF description exists
            add description
        END IF

        add publication date

        return card


    FUNCTION renderCards(posts)

        clear track

        FOR EACH post
            card = createCard(post)
            append card to track
        END FOR


    FUNCTION getVisibleCard()

        inspect cards

        determine most visible card

        return card


    FUNCTION scrollToCard(card)

        IF card does not exist
            return

        card.scrollIntoView(smooth + centered)


    FUNCTION setupNavigation()

        previous button click
            current = getVisibleCard()
            previous = card before current
            scrollToCard(previous)

        next button click
            current = getVisibleCard()
            next = card after current
            scrollToCard(next)


    FUNCTION setupActiveState()

        create IntersectionObserver

        observe cards

        when visibility changes
            determine most visible card
            update active class


    FUNCTION setupInteractions()

        setup navigation

        setup active state


    FUNCTION initialize(options)

        resolve container

        resolve track

        obtain blog posts

        validate posts

        IF no posts
            render empty state
            return controller

        renderCards(posts)

        setupInteractions()

        return controller


    EXPORT initBlogHorizontalScroll
One important architecture correction

I would not make blog-horizontal-scroll.js fetch post.md itself.

That would create this:

activity-blog.js
    ↓ fetch posts

blog-horizontal-scroll.js
    ↓ fetch same posts again

which is dumb duplication.

Instead:

                ┌── Activity Center
                │
Markdown ──→ activity-blog.js
                │
                └──→ blog-horizontal-scroll.js

Both components consume the same normalized blog data.

That also means later, when you move from:

Markdown

to:

FastAPI + database

you don't have to rewrite both UI modules. You replace the data source while keeping the rendering/interaction architecture intact.

That's the kind of separation that will save your ass six months from now when this project has inevitably grown twelve tentacles. 🫠

And importantly, this gives blog-horizontal-scroll.js a very clean contract:

INPUT:
normalized blog posts

OUTPUT:
interactive horizontal blog browser
